INSERT INTO lr_explanation_steps (explanation_id, position, label, body) SELECT e.id, v.position, v.label, v.body FROM lr_explanations e JOIN lr_questions q ON q.id=e.question_id CROSS JOIN (VALUES (0, $pilot$Statements$pilot$, $pilot$- Much of today's literature is inferior.
  - Most of today's authors are intellectually and emotionally inexperienced.
  - Their works lack intricacy and a focus on the significant, which are what characterize good literature.
  - *pivot (however)*
  - Hypatia's latest novel is promising: it shows maturity, complexity and grace far beyond her *earlier works*.$pilot$), (1, $pilot$Evaluate$pilot$, $pilot$When we read Inference paragraphs, we hunt for claims that can be combined, usually through an Overlapping Idea. We also watch for Conditional, Causal and Mathematical wording, and for a paragraph that Ends with a Pivot.

This one ends with a pivot, so our job is usually to reconcile the last idea with what came before it.

Where's the overlap? Good literature has *intricacy*. Hypatia's new novel has more *complexity*. Intricacy and complexity are close enough to be the same property. So her new novel has more of one of the things good literature has.

Now notice what Hypatia's novel is compared to. Not today's literature. Her own earlier books. That limits what we can say. And "promising" is the author holding back on purpose. Promising isn't good.$pilot$), (2, $pilot$Goal$pilot$, $pilot$We want a safely worded answer that combines the two halves: Hypatia's latest novel has more of a good-literature trait (complexity/intricacy) than her earlier work. Be suspicious of anything that ranks her against other authors or calls her novel good literature.$pilot$)) v(position,label,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT104-S4-Q23$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_steps s WHERE s.explanation_id=e.id) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lr_explanation_choice_notes (explanation_id, letter, body) SELECT e.id, v.letter, v.body FROM lr_explanations e JOIN lr_questions q ON q.id=e.question_id CROSS JOIN (VALUES ($pilot$A$pilot$, $pilot$We have no idea whether Hypatia's new novel focuses on the significant at all. The passage credits it with maturity, complexity and grace. "Focus on the significant" isn't on that list, so we can't say she beats anyone on that score.$pilot$), ($pilot$B$pilot$, $pilot$Today's works are said to lack intricacy and a focus on the significant. Grace only shows up in the description of Hypatia's novel. Nothing tells us the rest of today's literature lacks it.$pilot$), ($pilot$C$pilot$, $pilot$"Promising" is not "good." All we know is that the new book beats Hypatia's earlier books. If her earlier books were terrible, "much better than terrible" can still fall short of good literature.$pilot$), ($pilot$D$pilot$, $pilot$Two problems. The only comparison in the passage is to her *earlier* works, not to today's literature. And "much" of today's literature is inferior, which isn't the same as "the majority." A lot of trap answers take an absolute idea and turn it into a comparison the passage never made.$pilot$), ($pilot$E$pilot$, $pilot$This is the most safely worded answer available. It stays inside the one comparison we actually have (new novel vs. her earlier works) and only claims *at least one* property. Complexity is that property: it matches the intricacy that characterizes good literature, and her new novel has more of it.

On Most Supported, "at least one," "some" and "can" are good signs. The right answer usually claims just enough to be backed by the passage.$pilot$)) v(letter,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT104-S4-Q23$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_choice_notes n WHERE n.explanation_id=e.id) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

