  - Computers can't help with the higher intellectual functions (understanding). Only human teachers can.$pilot$), (2, $pilot$Evaluate$pilot$, $pilot$Morris's conclusion has a New Concept the evidence never mentions: an "appreciable impact on education." The evidence talks about drills and higher intellectual functions, but nothing says what it takes to have an appreciable impact.

So we need a bridge from what the evidence says about computers to the new concept:
```
can't help with higher    →   inappreciable impact
intellectual functions        on education
```
Or its contrapositive, which is an equally good correct answer:
```
appreciable impact    →   does something for higher
on education              intellectual functions
```
Either version finishes the job. If an appreciable impact requires doing something for the higher functions, and computers can't, computers can't have an appreciable impact.$pilot$), (3, $pilot$Goal$pilot$, $pilot$Look for an answer that connects "appreciable impact on education" to the higher intellectual functions, most likely as a requirement: an appreciable impact needs to affect students' understanding.$pilot$)) v(position,label,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT114-S4-Q12$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_steps s WHERE s.explanation_id=e.id) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lr_explanation_choice_notes (explanation_id, letter, body) SELECT e.id, v.letter, v.body FROM lr_explanations e JOIN lr_questions q ON q.id=e.question_id CROSS JOIN (VALUES ($pilot$A$pilot$, $pilot$This closes one door: computers don't even add anything to memorization. But it doesn't close all of them. Could computers still have an appreciable impact on education some other way (organizing information, motivating students, connecting students to teachers)? Nothing in (A) rules that out. Morris needs a rule that says the *only* road to an appreciable impact runs through the higher functions. (A) doesn't give us that.$pilot$), ($pilot$B$pilot$, $pilot$If lots of memorization actually *hurts* the development of higher functions, and computers push memorization, then computers might have a big (bad) impact on education. That's an appreciable impact, which works against Morris.$pilot$), ($pilot$C$pilot$, $pilot$This is the most popular wrong answer. If memorization is a necessary step toward higher functions, then computers, which help with memorization, might be helping indirectly with understanding after all. That opens a door to computers having an appreciable impact. Morris wants that door shut.$pilot$), ($pilot$D$pilot$, $pilot$When students first meet computers says nothing about the size of computers' impact on their education.$pilot$), ($pilot$E$pilot$, $pilot$This is the contrapositive version of our bridge:
```
appreciable impact    →   affects higher
on education              intellectual functions
```
Does the trigger apply? Take the contrapositive: if computers don't affect the higher functions, they don't have an appreciable impact. The evidence says computers can't *help* with those functions.

Watch the words. "Help" and "affect" are not quite the same: something could affect understanding by getting in its way. Morris's evidence only rules out helping. So (E) doesn't seal the conclusion with perfect force. It is still the only answer that ties "appreciable impact" to the higher functions, which is the gap the argument leaves open, and in context Morris is plainly using "affect" in the helping sense. Every other answer either fails to link the new concept or works against Morris. Pick the answer that closes the real gap, even when its wording is a shade looser than the evidence.

On Sufficient Assumption, when the conclusion has a concept the evidence never mentions, the right answer almost always defines that new concept in terms of something the evidence did cover.$pilot$)) v(letter,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT114-S4-Q12$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_choice_notes n WHERE n.explanation_id=e.id) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

