Conversationally, what's our objection?
  *Given that ...* it's wrong for one individual to hold all the power,
  *How could we argue that ...* it's fine for society as a whole to hold it?

Because groups can have properties their members don't. One person running the country might be wrong because it's one unchecked person. A whole society deciding together has debate, checks and majority votes. The very thing that made it wrong for the individual could be missing for the group.

That's the Famous Part vs. Whole flaw: what's true of each part is assumed to be true of the whole. One brick can't hold up a roof. Many bricks together can.$pilot$), (4, $pilot$Goal$pilot$, $pilot$Look for an answer we can hit with the same objection: "Sure, each member can't / shouldn't, but the group they form might." The direction matters: part to whole. Answers that go whole to part, or from one individual to other individuals, have a different flaw even if they feel similar.$pilot$)) v(position,label,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT104-S4-Q13$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_steps s WHERE s.explanation_id=e.id) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
